Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "grouping_reason": "Four Wazuh alerts correlated within a 56-second burst window (14:08:02 → 14:08:58) spanning two hosts on the same logical AD CS workflow: (1) two Sysmon Event 1 process-creation events on WS-ENG02.corp.local (CORP\\eng.user runs 'certutil.exe -template' then 'certutil.exe -pulse', parentImage=cmd.exe), (2) one Windows Security Event 4898 'Certificate Services template loaded' on CERT-SRV01.corp.local for template 'UserAuth', (3) one Windows Security Event 4899 'Certificate Services received a certificate request' on CERT-SRV01 — Requester=CORP\\eng.user, Template=UserAuth, Subject=CN=eng.user,OU=Engineering,DC=corp,DC=local, SerialNumber=5d:0a:81:7c:f1:e2:33:9b, RequestId=4127. Correlation rule: same user identity (CORP\\eng.user) spans both hosts within 60s + sequence is template-enumeration→template-load→cert-request which is the canonical AD CS abuse stage-1 fingerprint (MITRE T1087.002 + T1649). The DISTINGUISHING question this alert does NOT yet answer: is this a one-off legitimate enrollment by a developer who needs a code-signing cert, OR is this ESC1/ESC8 abuse stage 1 against an over-permissive cert template?",
  "severity": "high",
  "alert_count": 4,
  "unique_steps": 4,
  "time_window": {"start": "2026-08-14T14:08:02+0200", "end": "2026-08-14T14:08:58+0200"},
  "network_observations": [
    {"host": "WS-ENG02.corp.local", "ip": "10.40.7.22", "role": "engineering_workstation_certificate_enumeration_source"},
    {"host": "CERT-SRV01.corp.local", "ip": "10.40.2.11", "role": "internal_ad_cs_server_target"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "first_seen": "2026-08-14T14:08:02+0200", "tactic": "Discovery", "technique": "T1087.002", "what_happened": "On WS-ENG02.corp.local (10.40.7.22), user CORP\\eng.user spawned certutil.exe -template under parentImage=cmd.exe (parentCommandLine='C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe'). Sysmon Event 1 fired. certutil.exe -template enumerates every AD CS certificate template the user is permitted to enroll on — the canonical AD CS reconnaissance command used by Certify/Certipy operators to find ESC1/ESC4/ESC8-vulnerable templates.", "chain_reason": "first sighting of CORP\\eng.user invoking AD CS recon tooling — anchor of the 60s correlation window"},
    {"step": 2, "first_seen": "2026-08-14T14:08:15+0200", "tactic": "Discovery", "technique": "T1087.002", "what_happened": "Same WS-ENG02 host, same CORP\\eng.user, 13 seconds later: certutil.exe -pulse refreshes the local AD CS template cache by triggering autoenrollment. Sysmon Event 1 fired. This back-to-back -template / -pulse pair is signature behavior for an attacker validating that newly-discovered templates appear in their enrollment scope before issuing a request.", "chain_reason": "same user + same host + 13s gap, semantically chained to step 1 (recon → cache-refresh)"},
    {"step": 3, "first_seen": "2026-08-14T14:08:38+0200", "tactic": "Credential Access", "technique": "T1649", "what_happened": "On CERT-SRV01.corp.local (10.40.2.11, the enterprise CA), Windows Security Event 4898 'Certificate Services template loaded' fired for template name 'UserAuth', subject CORP\\eng.user. Event 4898 is logged the moment the CA processes a template lookup for a specific requester — it precedes the actual issuance and proves the user touched the template's load path.", "chain_reason": "same identity CORP\\eng.user pivots from workstation to CA server within 23 seconds — the user moved from local enumeration to active CA interaction"},
    {"step": 4, "first_seen": "2026-08-14T14:08:55+0200", "tactic": "Credential Access", "technique": "T1649", "what_happened": "17 seconds after the template load, same CERT-SRV01 fires Windows Security Event 4899 'Certificate Services received a certificate request': Requester=CORP\\eng.user, Template=UserAuth, Subject=CN=eng.user,OU=Engineering,DC=corp,DC=local, Cert SerialNumber=5d:0a:81:7c:f1:e2:33:9b, RequestId=4127. The cert is now in the CA queue — depending on the template's approval setting, issuance may be automatic.", "chain_reason": "completes the AD CS abuse stage-1 sequence: enum → cache-refresh → template-load → cert-request, all within 56s"}
  ]
}
